Consider this synchronous C# iterator:

```csharp
IEnumerable<int> Values(int count)
{
    if (count < 0)
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(count));

    yield return count;
}

IEnumerable<int> sequence;
try
{
    sequence = Values(-1);
}
catch (ArgumentOutOfRangeException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("caught");
    return;
}

foreach (var value in sequence)
    Console.WriteLine(value);
```

Why does the `catch` shown fail to catch the validation exception?

Consider this synchronous C# code:

```csharp
IEnumerable<int> Values()
{
    try
    {
        yield return 7;
        yield return 9;
    }
    finally
    {
        Console.WriteLine("released");
    }
}

foreach (var value in Values())
{
    throw new InvalidOperationException();
}
```

The caller catches the exception. Explain why `released` is printed before the exception reaches that caller, although the sequence was not exhausted.
